1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,760
and they'll have some difficult decisions to make.

2
00:00:02,760 --> 00:00:07,440
It's tempting to offer up general decision-making principles like minimize harm,

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:11,240
but even that quickly leads to morally murky decisions.

4
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:14,160
For example, let's say we have the same initial setup,

5
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,360
but now there's a motorcyclist wearing a helmet to your left

6
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:20,040
and another one without a helmet to your right.

7
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:23,280
Which one should your robot car crash into?

8
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:27,240
If you say the biker with the helmet because she's more likely to survive,

9
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:30,280
then aren't you penalizing the responsible motorist?

10
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:34,920
If instead you say the biker without the helmet because he's acting irresponsibly,

11
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:39,640
then you've gone way beyond the initial design principle about minimizing harm,

12
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:44,160
and the robot car is now meeting out street justice.

13
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:47,120
The ethical considerations get more complicated here.

14
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:53,080
In both of our scenarios, the underlying design is functioning as a targeting algorithm of sorts.

15
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:56,720
In other words, it's systematically favoring or discriminating

16
00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:00,520
against a certain type of object to crash into.

17
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:05,600
And the owners of the target vehicles will suffer the negative consequences of this algorithm

18
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:07,760
through no fault of their own.

19
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:12,040
Our new technologies are opening up many other novel ethical dilemmas.

20
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:18,360
For instance, if you had to choose between a car that would always save as many lives as possible in an accident,

21
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:22,720
or one that would save you at any cost, which would you buy?

22
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:27,560
What happens if the cars start analyzing and factoring in the passengers of the cars

23
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:29,880
and the particulars of their lives?

24
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:34,840
Could it be the case that a random decision is still better than a predetermined one

25
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:36,920
designed to minimize harm?

26
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:39,560
And who should be making all of these decisions anyhow?

27
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:40,480
Programmers?

28
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:41,400
Companies?

29
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:42,800
Governments?

30
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:47,760
Reality may not play out exactly like our thought experiments, but that's not the point.

31
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:52,360
They're designed to isolate and stress test our intuitions on ethics,

32
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:55,880
just like science experiments do for the physical world.

33
00:01:55,880 --> 00:02:02,600
Spotting these moral hairpin turns now will help us maneuver the unfamiliar road of technology ethics

34
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:08,640
and allow us to cruise confidently and conscientiously into our brave new future.

